{"qid":"Q1009","names":["Republic of Cameroon"],"title":"Republic of Cameroon","endonym":{"native":"République du Cameroun","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Republic of Cameroon gained independence from French administration on 1 January 1960, becoming one of the first sub-Saharan African states to do so under the United Nations trusteeship system. The southern portion of British Cameroons voted in a 1961 plebiscite to join the French-speaking republic, forming the Federal Republic of Cameroon, which was restructured into a unitary state in 1972. Under President Paul Biya, in power since 1982, the country has maintained political stability at the cost of democratic constraints, while an armed conflict between the anglophone northwest and southwest regions and government forces has persisted since 2016.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Cameroon's international borders are treaty-defined and well established, with the International Court of Justice resolving the Bakassi Peninsula dispute with Nigeria in 2002; internal administrative boundaries are clearly documented.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Cameroon","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Cameroon"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Cameroon","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cameroon"},{"label":"Victor Julius Ngoh, History of Cameroon Since 1800 (Presbook, 1996)","url":"https://archive.org/details/historyofcamroon0000ngoh"}],"britannica":"place/Cameroon","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Cameroon","rulers":[{"qid":"Q298718","name":"Ahmadou Ahidjo","start":1960,"end":1982,"image":"Ahmadou Ahidjo visits the White House on July 26, 1982.jpg","imageUrl":